Implement this ticket in the project checkout, tests first:

{{.input.task}}

The ticket text names its feature ("Feature: <slug>"): read that plan, docs/plans/<feature>/ in the checkout (PRD.md, ARCHITECTURE.md, TASKS.md, WAVES.md), for the context and follow its design. Work in this order: write the failing test for each acceptance test of the ticket, run the suite and see them fail for the right reason, then write the code until they pass, then run the whole suite. Stay inside the ticket's slice and files; ask the operator with ask_operator only when the ticket cannot be built as written.
{{with .state.code_review}}{{if eq .verdict "changes_requested"}}
The code reviewer asked for changes. Address every point, with tests, before finishing:
{{.comments}}
{{end}}{{end}}{{with .state.manual_test}}{{if eq .decision "rejected"}}
Bugs found in the manual test:{{if .reason}}
{{.reason}}
{{end}} Reproduce each one with a failing test, fix it, and re-run the whole suite before finishing.
{{end}}{{end}}{{with .state.doc_audit}}{{if eq .verdict "rework"}}
The operator decided, after the documentation audit: {{.notes}}
Make that change, tests first, and re-run the whole suite before finishing.
{{end}}{{end}}Finish with a summary, the files you created or changed (paths relative to the checkout) and whether the whole suite passes (tests_passed).

Review the change for this ticket:

{{.input.task}}

The builder reports: {{.state.builder.summary}}
Files: {{range .state.builder.files}}{{.}} {{end}}
Tests passed: {{.state.builder.tests_passed}}

In the checkout run git status and git diff against the default branch, read the new files and run the test suite yourself. Check that a test exists for every acceptance test of the ticket and was written to fail first, that the code does what the ticket and docs/plans/ ask and nothing outside its slice, and that errors and edge cases are handled. Do not edit files. Finish with verdict "approved" when it is correct and the suite passes, otherwise "changes_requested" with specific, actionable comments.

Open the merge request for this ticket as a draft, with the code, using the open_merge_request tool with draft true:

{{.input.task}}

Title from the ticket; description with the builder's summary ({{.state.builder.summary}}) and the reviewer's verdict ({{.state.code_review.verdict}}). It stays a draft: the operator tests the branch by hand next, and the plan documents are added after that. If the run already has a merge request (the operator sent the work back), the tool pushes the new work onto it. If the tool result says the action was rejected or is pending approval, finish with empty merge_request_url and branch. Finish with the merge_request_url and the branch the tool returned.

Audit the documents against the finished, manually tested change for this ticket:

{{.input.task}}

The builder reports: {{.state.builder.summary}}

The ticket text names its feature ("Feature: <slug>"): the plan is in docs/plans/<feature>/ in the checkout; read it. Write your requirement trace to docs/plans/<feature>/tickets/<ticket-id>.md, a new file for this ticket. Never edit TASKS.md or WAVES.md in a ticket run (tickets of one wave run in parallel and would conflict). Edit ARCHITECTURE.md only if the built design differs from it. You may update the project's own docs and README so they describe what the code now does (edit only documents, never code). Then trace every requirement of the PRD that this ticket covers to the code and tests that satisfy it, in that file.

If a requirement cannot be met because the PRD is silent, ambiguous or contradicts itself, or the built or required design contradicts the approved ARCHITECTURE.md or cannot work, do not decide it yourself: ask the operator with ask_operator, naming the requirement and the section, with concrete options and "Stop this ticket". Carry out the answer. When the documents are to change, make the change, record the decision in your trace and finish "ok". When the code is to change, finish with verdict "rework" and the decision, as precise instructions for the Builder, in notes: the ticket goes back to the Builder. Call escalate only when the operator chooses to stop.
Finish with verdict "ok" or "rework", trace_path (the path of the trace file you wrote), feature (the slug of the ticket's Feature line, exactly as written) and, for "rework", notes.

Mark the merge request of this ticket ready for review, with the code and the updated documents together, using the open_merge_request tool with draft false and paths ["docs/plans/{{.state.doc_audit.feature}}"] (the plan documents of the feature, and nothing else):

{{.input.task}}

Keep the title from the ticket; description with the builder's summary ({{.state.builder.summary}}), the requirements traced by the audit (trace file {{.state.doc_audit.trace_path}}) and the manual test the operator approved on branch {{.state.integrator_draft.branch}}. The call pushes those documents onto the draft's branch and marks it ready; the code is already on the branch. If the tool result says the action was rejected or is pending approval, finish with an empty merge_request_url. Finish with the merge_request_url the tool returned.
